WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.080
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday! It's Question
Tuesday, the day that I answer real questions

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:04.770
from real Nerdfighters. Let's get right to
it!

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.930
I'll be meeting my boyfriend's mother at Christmas
and she only speaks German. How do I make

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:10.220
this less awkward?
Well you should learn a little German. I'm

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:13.240
sure there's some German Nerdfighters in comments
who can help you. But I can start, here's

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.030
a good sentence: "Das Schicksal ist ein mieser
Verräter." Here's another one "Ihr Sohn ist

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:18.070
ein guter Küsser."

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:22.410
Can you say anything in Spanish?
Can I? Of course! "Me llamo Juan Verde. Soy

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:23.410
un nerd."

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:24.560
Favorite video game?
Original Super Mario Kart.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.750
Hey John, how are you today? Also, do you
have any book recommendations?

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:30.220
I'm good. I'm a little bit annoyed about the
overall quality of political discourse in

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.410
the United States. But yes! I do have book
recommendations. I'm just re-reading Life

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:37.870
on the Mississippi by Mark Twain which is
so good! Also, I just read Toni Morrison's

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.460
new novel, and I know it's not her best book
or anything, but I still thought it was great!

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.399
Do you have any unpopular opinions about classic
literature?

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:46.480
I kind of stand with W. H. Auden on this one.
"Some books are undeservedly forgotten, none

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.059
are undeservedly remembered."

00:00:48.059 --> 00:00:50.660
Is there anything you aren't terrified of
that you should be?

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.640
No. I don't want to brag, but I sort of specialize
in being terrified.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.820
Do you think everyone should consider taking
a hiatus from social media like you have?

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.180
I don't know, but I can say I haven't missed
social media as much as I thought I would.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.860
It had become a really unhealthy place for
me in a lot of ways. For the last, like, 8

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:07.700
years I've been surrounded by a community
online that is incredibly generous and kind

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.969
to me, and to my work. And criticism has almost
always been really thoughtful and respectful.

00:01:11.969 --> 00:01:16.619
But there's been this small, but persistent
amount of really vicious and hateful stuff

00:01:16.619 --> 00:01:21.859
directed at me that I just had found tremendously
hurtful, and it hasn't been good for my mental

00:01:21.859 --> 00:01:25.840
health, and... yeah. And while there are definitely
things that I miss about my old internet life,

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.259
I find it much easier to write and do other
work without all of that stuff floating around

00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:31.259
in my head.

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:32.259
Speaking of writing:

00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:33.829
What's your new book about?
I can't say because I don't know which parts

00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:37.399
of what I'm writing is going to actually end
up in a book, but I did put a clue for you

00:01:37.399 --> 00:01:41.210
in this video! But you won't be able to decode
the clue until after you read the story, so

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:42.319
it's kind of useless I'm sorry.

00:01:42.319 --> 00:01:45.219
Did you ever read Henning Mankell?
Yes, of course, Mankell was a great Swedish

00:01:45.219 --> 00:01:48.799
crime novelist who sadly died yesterday. I
always admired him, not just for his stories

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:52.599
but also because he made a lot of money and
gave almost all of it away. He once wrote,

00:01:52.599 --> 00:01:56.929
"I still have a photo on my wall of the greatest
idol I will ever have, myself at age 8. Because

00:01:56.929 --> 00:02:00.809
that's when the forces of the imagination
have the same value as the real world, when

00:02:00.809 --> 00:02:04.929
they're an instrument of survival. When my
mother disappeared, and I imagined a mother,

00:02:04.929 --> 00:02:06.130
that was me at my best."

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.649
What's your signature dish?
You know, there's that, like, small plate

00:02:08.649 --> 00:02:12.569
that's for desserts and there's that big plate
that's for dinner, and then there's that medium

00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:18.040
sized plate that's sort of all-purpose? I
am a huge fan of this medium plate, my signature

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:19.040
dish.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.540
Would you be interested in becoming the next
manager of Liverpool Football Club?

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.650
Oh yeah, I already sent them a resumé. My
qualifications include having taken AFC Wimbledon

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:30.650
from League 2 to the Champions League in FIFA
14, and having an inside track on one of the

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.370
world's best youth players, Henry Green.

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:35.459
Is there an animal you'd like to see in the
wild?

00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:37.030
I'm pretty into tuatara lately.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:39.959
How prepared do you feel for your drag race
against Maggie Stiefvater on Friday?

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:43.599
Well I actually have a friend who's a professional
racecar driver, so I consulted with her on

00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:48.349
this topic and she said, "John, you really,
you need to be prepared to lose." And so,

00:02:48.349 --> 00:02:50.439
in that sense, I guess that I'm very prepared.

00:02:50.439 --> 00:02:53.850
Would you rather be a sheep or a goat?
Definitely a sheep. I've always wanted to

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.530
be a sheep, because then when people are like
"Hey, where'd you get that sweater?" I could

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:00.040
be like "Oh, I knit it myself, using my own
hair."

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.180
What's the most impressive word you use in
casual conversation?

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:05.959
I use the word eschatological a lot, because
there's no shorter word that means the exact

00:03:05.959 --> 00:03:09.730
same thing and kind of my default emotional
state is eschatological anxiety.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:12.019
What's your best advice for travelling with
children?

00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:13.730
When possible, don't.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:15.840
What's the greenest green you've ever seen,
John Green?

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.670
This forest in Brazil when it was raining,
and everything was impossibly green, but everything

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:24.430
in a slightly different shade; it was really,
it was like home For Greens.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:28.269
If you could travel anywhere you wanted and
live there for a year, where would it be?

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:32.760
Honestly, if I could stay anywhere on Earth
for a year right now, it would be this house.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:36.230
Hey, who's your style icon?
I think you mean Styles icon.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.860
If you could be stuck in the world of any
TV show or movie, what would it be?

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.499
Star Trek: The Next Generation. Please let
that happen! Please let that happen! Please

00:03:41.499 --> 00:03:45.780
let me open my eyes and be hanging out with
Wesley Crusher. 3, 2, 1! DANGIT!

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.310
Hank, I suspect when it comes to that last
question, you and I are in agreement. I will

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:52.809
see you on Friday! Both at NerdCon and on
YouTube.

